Review and Complete the AI Vulnerability Remediation Automation Project

I have already created a project and generated some initial files using GitHub Copilot.

I want to build an AI-powered vulnerability remediation application using Python, React, Git, RTC, Veracode and Black Duck.

Your task is to review the existing implementation, identify missing functionality, and complete the application without unnecessarily rewriting the existing code.

1. Analyze the existing project

Before making changes:

- Read the existing README.md, AGENTS.md, configuration files, Python files and React components.
- Understand the current architecture and implemented functionality.
- Compare the implementation against all the requirements below.
- Identify which requirements are fully implemented, partially implemented or missing.
- Identify the relevant files and explain what needs to be changed.
- Reuse existing modules and components wherever possible.

After completing the analysis, implement the missing functionality.

Do not create duplicate files, duplicate modules or unnecessary new architecture.

2. Excel upload and vulnerability processing

The application must allow the user to upload an Excel file containing multiple vulnerabilities.

For example, the Excel file may contain 21 vulnerabilities.

The application must:

- Read the vulnerability ID, description, severity, affected file, version information and other available fields.
- Create a separate remediation record for every vulnerability.
- Maintain an independent status, workflow history and evidence for each vulnerability.
- Allow the user to track all vulnerabilities from a single dashboard.

3. Reproduce the vulnerability before fixing it

Before suggesting or applying a code fix, the application must attempt to reproduce the vulnerability.

The user must be able to provide the authorized development URL of the application being tested.

For example, the user may provide the LSR application development URL.

The application must:

- Identify the relevant test scenario based on the vulnerability description.
- Access the configured development environment using an approved browser automation tool.
- Execute the reproduction scenario.
- Capture screenshots showing the reproduced vulnerability.
- Save the reproduction results, test steps, timestamp and screenshots against the corresponding vulnerability ID.

If the vulnerability cannot be reproduced, the application must display the reason and allow the human to review the finding.

Do not assume that a vulnerability is reproduced merely because the application URL is accessible.

Do not proceed with an automatic fix when the reproduction result is inconclusive and reproduction is required.

4. AI-based vulnerability analysis and fix generation

After the initial reproduction attempt:

- Analyze the affected source code.
- Identify the root cause of the vulnerability.
- Generate a minimal and appropriate code fix.
- Explain why the change addresses the vulnerability.
- Identify the affected files and methods.
- Generate relevant test cases.
- Run the available tests and configured checks.

The AI must not blindly modify business functionality.

For risky changes, unclear business rules or uncertain fixes, pause the workflow and request human intervention.

5. Human intervention through the UI

The application must provide a mechanism for human intervention directly through the React dashboard.

When human intervention is required:

- Pause the workflow for the particular vulnerability.
- Display the vulnerability details, AI explanation, proposed fix, reproduction evidence and reason for requesting review.
- Allow the human to provide additional instructions, suggestions or corrections through the UI.
- Allow the AI to use the human's input to generate a revised fix.
- Display the revised changes for review.

The application must preserve the workflow state while waiting for human input.

After receiving the human's response, resume from the same vulnerability without restarting the entire workflow.

6. Individual vulnerability dashboard and evidence

For every vulnerability, provide a dedicated detailed view containing the following tabs or sections:

Vulnerability details

Display the vulnerability ID, description, severity, affected file and current remediation status.

Before & After Evidence

Display the following information:

- Before-fix screenshot.
- Before-fix reproduction result.
- Test scenario and steps.
- After-fix screenshot.
- After-fix verification result.
- Test execution timestamps.

Allow the user to view the before-and-after screenshots side by side and open them at full size.

Each screenshot and test result must be associated with the correct vulnerability ID and remediation attempt.

Do not fabricate screenshots or claim that a vulnerability has been reproduced or fixed without actual test evidence.

Suggested Changes

Display:

- AI explanation of the vulnerability.
- Proposed code changes.
- Affected files.
- Test results.
- Any warnings or risks identified by AI.

Allow the user to review the changes before committing.

Build and Security Results

Display the RTC build status, build number, Veracode findings and Black Duck findings associated with the remediation attempt.

7. Commit and push workflow

The application must use a dedicated remediation branch.

When the user reviews the proposed changes and clicks the Commit button:

- Validate that the configured checks have passed.
- Commit the approved changes to the dedicated branch.
- Push the changes to the remote repository.
- Never commit directly to a protected or main branch.
- Display the commit ID and push status in the UI.

The application must not commit or push unapproved changes.

The workflow must handle Git failures without losing the current remediation state.

8. Automatic RTC build

After a successful commit and push:

- Automatically trigger the RTC build for the correct branch.
- Track the build status.
- Display the build number, build result and relevant logs in the UI.
- Continue to security validation only after the required build completes successfully.

Do not assume that RTC supports a particular API or integration.

Inspect the available configuration and document the supported mechanism required to trigger builds programmatically.

If the current environment does not support automatic RTC triggering, implement a clearly defined integration interface and report what configuration or access is still required.

9. Veracode and Black Duck validation

After the RTC build completes successfully:

- Retrieve the corresponding Veracode and Black Duck scan reports.
- Compare the new findings against the original vulnerability list.
- Identify resolved, unresolved and newly introduced findings.
- Display the results in the UI.
- Associate each result with the correct vulnerability and build.

If vulnerabilities remain:

- Allow AI to analyze the remaining findings.
- Generate another proposed fix.
- Repeat the review, commit, build and validation process as appropriate.

The application must not mark a vulnerability as resolved solely because AI believes the fix is correct.

The final resolution status must be based on the configured security validation results.

10. Multiple vulnerability processing

The application must support processing multiple vulnerabilities independently.

For example, if the uploaded Excel contains 21 vulnerabilities:

- Create 21 separate remediation records.
- Track the status of each vulnerability independently.
- Maintain separate before-and-after screenshots and test results.
- Maintain separate proposed changes, commit references and build results.
- Allow one vulnerability to be paused for human intervention while other eligible vulnerabilities continue processing.
- Provide an overall dashboard showing the status of all 21 vulnerabilities.

The application should support sequential processing initially and allow safe parallel processing in the future.

Do not combine unrelated vulnerability fixes into one commit unless explicitly configured and approved.

11. Workflow safety and reliability

Implement the following safeguards:

- Use only explicitly configured and authorized development or test environments.
- Never run vulnerability reproduction tests against production environments.
- Use approved test accounts and test data.
- Do not perform destructive tests without explicit authorization.
- Keep credentials in environment variables or an approved secrets-management system.
- Never expose credentials in logs, screenshots or generated reports.
- Implement proper error handling, retry handling and workflow-state persistence.
- Prevent duplicate commits and duplicate build triggers.
- Preserve the workflow state when the application restarts.
- Require human review for risky or uncertain security changes.

12. Expected implementation

The final application should provide the following complete workflow:

Excel upload → Vulnerability analysis → Before-fix reproduction → Screenshot capture → AI fix generation → Human intervention when required → Code review → Commit and push → RTC build → Veracode and Black Duck validation → After-fix reproduction → Screenshot capture → Final remediation status.

13. Final response

After reviewing and implementing the missing functionality:

- Summarize what was already implemented.
- List the functionality that was missing.
- List the files created or modified.
- Explain how the complete workflow operates.
- Identify any external configuration, API access, credentials or permissions still required.
- Explain how to run the application and test the complete demo.

Do not claim that a feature is fully implemented unless the actual code supports it.

Prioritize a working end-to-end demonstration over unnecessary architectural complexity.
